1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,000
A customer key is a padded string here, an integer there,
and an unsafe float elsewhere. Valid SQL can still be wrong.

2
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:34,000
SchemaBridge reads bounded schemas and governance context from DataHub.
Missing evidence stays missing; it is never invented.

3
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:56,000
It proposes one Customer key with evidence, confidence,
normalization, and risk. A human still approves the mapping.

4
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The approved Customer to AccountHolder join is one-to-many.
Entity counts require the contract's exact distinct-key mitigation.

5
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The request counts customers by registration date
when the holder role is secondary.

6
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:55,000
A typed plan selects approved versions. A deterministic compiler
produces PostgreSQL, then an independent AST guard validates it.

7
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The read-only preview returns 2, 1, and 1.
Fractional, non-finite, and null identifiers are rejected visibly.

8
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:39,000
With explicit approval, SchemaBridge writes governed context
and a SQL-free recipe back to DataHub with target-level audit facts.

9
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:51,000
A later workflow retrieves the context but still replans,
recompiles, validates, and asks for execution approval.

10
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
SchemaBridge: govern the semantic layer
before asking an agent to query it.
